iagnosis and local correction, but dataset quality is determined by\nL883@P15: re-execution and subtree validation: a branch contributes supervision only if it yields a more viable\nL884@P15: continuation under the same stage conditions.\nL950@P17: as Expert Rollout Distillation (B = 2), we generate two candidate actions per expanded state. We\nL951@P17: use the same tree-expansion control mechanism as in our method (see Appendix B.1) and select\nL952@P17: supervision with the same subtree-success score.\nL953@P17: Best-of-N Distillation. This baseline extends rollout distillation by allowing the collector to retry\nL954@P17: a failed subgoal up to N times. We use N = 10: when a subgoal fails, generation restarts from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nO-VAD: Industrial Video Anomaly Detection through Object-Centric Tracking and Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2607.18142v1)\nciteturn34view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29view1\",\"pattern\":\"whole\"}); Total lines: 998\nL511: #### Object deduplication (whole vs. parts).\nL512: Stage 1 issues a single open-vocabulary VLM query requesting all distinct objects, and SAM is invoked with the full name list as a multi-class query that produces one fused binary mask. Whole and part labels (e.g., “screw” and “screw thread”) therefore collapse into a single connected mask region rather than spawning two tubelets. The textual labels are retained verbatim only as prompts for downstream per-tubelet identification, not as identity counts.\n"
